<meta type=article title="Caption-as-content + unified frameable helper (Phase 3 slice 3c)">
<author name="Test">
</author>
</meta>

<section | Caption as child tag>

Phase 3 slice 3c implements caption-as-content (Option A from the
Phase 0 findings): `<caption>` is a child tag position on every
frameable element, with the `caption=` kwarg lifting to child-tag
form at the normalize-to-canonical gate. Both authoring forms produce
the same Layer 1 AST.

A figure with the canonical child-tag caption form, exercising
formatted content (emphasis, **bold**) — impossible with the
kwarg-only shape:

<fig src=elephant.jpg>
<caption | An African elephant, photographed in *Serengeti National Park*.>
</fig>

The kwarg form (lifts to child-tag at the gate — produces identical
output for plain-text captions):

<fig #fig:zebra src=zebra.jpg caption="A zebra in the savanna." />

A frame with a title and caption, both rendered at the right edges
of the frame:

<frame +numbered #fig:method title="Methodology">
We used a forest of 500 decision trees trained on the cleaned
input data, with leave-one-out cross-validation for hyperparameter
search.
<caption | A summary of our **methods** section.>
</frame>

Cross-reference resolves: <ref @fig:zebra> → figure 1, <ref @fig:method>
→ figure 2 (the frame opted in via +numbered).

<section | Frame numbering>

Frames are numbered by default (#272), like figures and tables. A one-off
callout opts out of the figure counter with `-numbered`, so no number registers:

<frame -numbered | A bordered callout that doesn't participate in the figure counter.>

This frame is numbered (the default) and cross-referenceable via its id:

<frame #fig:opt-in caption="Numbered frame">
Body content here.
</frame>

The numbered frame is figure 4 in the document figure sequence.

<section | Title wiring>

The slice-3b frameable surface declared `title=` but didn't wire it.
This slice consumes it via the unified renderFrameable helper.

<fig title="Figure title" src=plot.png | A plot caption.>

Title above; caption below; image in the middle.
